    <!-- The sound driver is a Z80 program kept as it is in the cartridge (6798 bytes). The game's 68000 copies it to the
         Z80's RAM, releases the Z80 from reset, puts three set-up entries into the driver's request ring (Z80 RAM 0xE0, two
         bytes each: a value and a command code; the 68000 moves the write position in 0x09, the driver the read
         position in 0x08), and much later (warmup_frames) a request of command code 0 with the number of a song
         or sound as its value. Requests 0x00-0x05 are songs, 0x06-0x26 sounds (0x06, 0x07 and 0x19-0x1F are
         sampled sounds played through the YM2612's DAC, the others use FM and the PSG). Numbers above 0x26 read past the end of the
         driver's table and stop the driver, so they are not listed. The driver has no request that silences
         everything: Music Stop starts the Machine again. This is a prototype of the game: several of the
         sounds are a single short blip (0x08, 0x0A, 0x0B, 0x0E, 0x10, 0x11, 0x13-0x17, 0x20, 0x24-0x26). -->
